1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:05,297
Trurl bought an old book
from a passing peddler.

2
00:00:05,337 --> 00:00:09,460
It described a tunnel through a burning
star, and beyond it a black waste

3
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:13,491
called the Shroud, where a nameless
monster lies in wait for caravans.

4
00:00:14,291 --> 00:00:17,649
TRURL: Look. There is even an
engraving of it, eating two ships.

5
00:00:18,449 --> 00:00:23,676
KLAPAUCIUS: I could draw you a dragon right
now, with a thousand suns for each eye.

6
00:00:23,716 --> 00:00:26,191
Would the drawing prove that it exists?

7
00:00:26,791 --> 00:00:31,570
Your Shroud is a cosmic dump. It
is dark because of the garbage.

8
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:33,532
TRURL: We will go there.

9
00:00:33,572 --> 00:00:36,627
Then you will see what is true
in this book, and what is not.

10
00:00:45,827 --> 00:00:48,775
The Wastes were not a
shroud. They were a junkyard.

11
00:00:48,815 --> 00:00:52,388
Old stars, cinders, cans and
wrecks, all clattering together.

12
00:00:53,388 --> 00:00:57,665
Sand pattered on the bow. Then the ship
lurched, and everything went flying.

13
00:01:01,065 --> 00:01:03,149
It stuck nose first in something soft.

14
00:01:03,189 --> 00:01:06,766
Outside it was pitch black, and
someone was banging on the hull.

15
00:01:14,166 --> 00:01:17,628
A face filled the hatch. It
was covered with bulging eyes.

16
00:01:17,668 --> 00:01:20,587
The nose was a saw, and
the jaw was an iron hook.

17
00:01:20,627 --> 00:01:23,646
The eyes looked everything
over, as if pricing it.

18
00:01:24,646 --> 00:01:29,372
TRURL: Well? What do you want, you unwashed
mug? We have no gold and no silver.

19
00:01:29,412 --> 00:01:30,619
Take that face away!

20
00:01:31,819 --> 00:01:37,058
PUGG: I have no use for gold or
silver. Speak to me delicately.

21
00:01:37,098 --> 00:01:39,277
I am a pirate with a Ph.D.

22
00:01:39,877 --> 00:01:45,946
I rob in a modern and scientific
manner. I collect facts. Genuine truths.

23
00:01:45,986 --> 00:01:47,743
Information of value.

24
00:01:48,343 --> 00:01:52,137
Hand it over, or I
whistle. One. Two. Three.

25
00:01:52,637 --> 00:01:54,130
Four. Five.

26
00:01:58,930 --> 00:02:02,921
You don’t like my face?
Maybe you will like my hand.

27
00:02:10,921 --> 00:02:15,252
Come to terms now, or I will
put you at the bottom of my bin,

28
00:02:15,292 --> 00:02:17,728
under the rocks, and let you rust.

29
00:02:18,328 --> 00:02:21,640
KLAPAUCIUS: What exactly does
Your Doctoral Diplomahood want?

30
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:27,804
PUGG: Everything is information. I
want all of it, as long as it is true.

31
00:02:27,844 --> 00:02:32,582
For false information, as for
false coin, I give a good beating.

32
00:02:33,382 --> 00:02:37,907
KLAPAUCIUS: He could keep us here an eon
before we told him everything we know.

33
00:02:40,107 --> 00:02:44,211
TRURL: Then take the best fact we
have. How to make gold out of hydrogen.

34
00:02:44,811 --> 00:02:47,030
One hundred and ninety-six atoms.

35
00:02:47,070 --> 00:02:52,411
Shell the electrons, knead the protons,
sprinkle the electrons back on. Watch.

36
00:02:58,011 --> 00:03:00,067
PUGG: Yes, that is gold.

37
00:03:00,567 --> 00:03:03,116
TRURL: You could turn the
whole universe into gold!

38
00:03:03,916 --> 00:03:08,215
PUGG: If the universe were
gold, gold would be worthless.

39
00:03:09,015 --> 00:03:10,466
TRURL: Then what do you want to know?

40
00:03:11,066 --> 00:03:12,224
PUGG: Everything!

41
00:03:15,224 --> 00:03:19,373
Trurl promised him a Demon of the
Second Kind, if he swore to let them go.

42
00:03:19,413 --> 00:03:19,995
Pugg swore.

43
00:03:20,035 --> 00:03:24,637
He led them down through his cellars, past
sacks of stolen facts tied with string,

44
00:03:24,677 --> 00:03:27,960
and a great catalog chained
to the rock, beginning with A.

45
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:32,305
PUGG: There is air down
here. It is not very clean.

46
00:03:33,105 --> 00:03:34,683
TRURL: It does not matter.

47
00:03:34,723 --> 00:03:39,578
Air is atoms, jumping this way and that,
colliding billions of times a second.

48
00:03:39,978 --> 00:03:41,107
They jump at random.

49
00:03:41,147 --> 00:03:43,446
But there are so many that now and then,

50
00:03:43,486 --> 00:03:46,175
by accident, they make
something with meaning.

51
00:03:46,575 --> 00:03:50,915
As if you fired at a wall blindfold,
and the holes spelled a letter.

52
00:03:50,955 --> 00:03:54,780
For every sentence of sense
there are thousands of nonsense,

53
00:03:54,820 --> 00:03:56,559
and both fly apart at once.

54
00:03:57,159 --> 00:04:01,444
So the whole trick is a selector,
that keeps only what means something.

55
00:04:01,484 --> 00:04:03,738
That is the Demon of the Second Kind.

56
00:04:04,538 --> 00:04:07,022
PUGG: And what is the First Kind?

57
00:04:07,522 --> 00:04:09,467
KLAPAUCIUS: An ordinary demon.

58
00:04:09,507 --> 00:04:13,040
It lets the fast atoms out
and keeps the slow ones in.

59
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:15,594
It has nothing to do with information.

60
00:04:17,794 --> 00:04:21,154
Pugg found an old iron barrel,
and made a tiny hole in it.

61
00:04:21,194 --> 00:04:24,595
The air inside was so foul
that one’s nose wanted to hide.

62
00:04:25,395 --> 00:04:28,557
Trurl set the Demon astride
the hole, a mote of a mite

63
00:04:28,597 --> 00:04:31,976
with a diamond pen, and threaded
the paper tape under it.

64
00:04:35,376 --> 00:04:39,543
The pen began to scratch, like a
telegraph, only a million times faster.

65
00:04:39,583 --> 00:04:43,965
Pugg sat down by the barrel, lifted
the tape to his hundred eyes, and read.

66
00:04:46,365 --> 00:04:48,854
He did not notice the
two constructors leave.

67
00:04:48,894 --> 00:04:52,602
They pulled the ship once, twice,
and the third time it came free.

68
00:04:57,202 --> 00:05:00,130
They knew the Demon would
work. It would work too well.

69
00:05:02,330 --> 00:05:03,960
He read about everything.

70
00:05:21,130 --> 00:05:25,119
All of it was true. All of it meant
something. All of it was useless.

71
00:05:28,730 --> 00:05:30,923
PUGG: Enough! I have had enough!

72
00:05:32,330 --> 00:05:34,778
But the tape had wrapped
him in its paper miles,

73
00:05:34,818 --> 00:05:37,151
and he had too many eyes
not to keep reading.

74
00:05:47,330 --> 00:05:50,240
He sits there to this day,
at the bottom of his rubbish,

75
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:53,546
under a mountain of paper, and
the diamond pen still flickers.

76
00:05:55,930 --> 00:05:58,548
He learns about rickshaws,
rents and roaches,

77
00:05:58,588 --> 00:06:01,442
and about his own fate,
which has been told here.

78
00:06:01,482 --> 00:06:02,900
That is on the tape too.

79
00:06:08,130 --> 00:06:11,288
There is no hope for him.
Unless the paper runs out.

